The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The reports say otherwise.
Run the desk's own cause logic over everything merged into #4471 and three problems come out,
each with its own ticket in the tracker repo:

1. **The shared parser reads dates month first.** Tenants here write the day first, so a typed
   date lands in the wrong month. This is the bug #4471 knows about, and most reports belong
   to it. Engineering fixed it: #4471 closed 05 October with `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
   locales`. → [tracker ticket #1](https://github.com/damian-kolakowski/support-kb-4471/issues/1),
   closed as fixed
2. **The booking-v3 form's date hint is wrong.** Under its date field it prints `MM/DD/YYYY`,
   people type what the hint says, and the form reads it day first. Same symptom, different
   bug and different fix: every affected report comes from the three booking-v3 tenants, through
   the form. Engineering will not fix it, because the booking-v3 form is retired.
   → [tracker ticket #2](https://github.com/damian-kolakowski/support-kb-4471/issues/2),
   closed as won't fix
3. **Some bookings move by one day, not one month.** The desk footnoted these while merging:
   June-only shifts, a Lisbon and a Toronto reader seeing different days for one meeting, an
   invite dated before its booking. That is time-zone or DST handling, and no date parser fix
   will touch it. The engineering update on #4471 decided the other two causes and says nothing
   about this one, so it is still open.
   → [tracker ticket #3](https://github.com/damian-kolakowski/support-kb-4471/issues/3), open

<Callout>
Not one issue. Three, merged under one subject, because
[decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone and every date
bug gets the same subject. The counts below are computed from the export at render time.
</Callout>

## The split, computed

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Intake's tag and the real cause disagree on dozens of reports, because intake guesses from
the reporter's browser and the cause depends on the tenant's locale and form. That disagreement
is why reading the table beats trusting the tags.

## Where the three stand

- Ticket 1 is fixed. #4471's own fix closes this cause and nothing else: the count that fix earns
  is 374 reports, not 412.
- Ticket 2 is closed as won't fix, because the form it describes is retired. The 33 reports under
  it are answered, not fixed.
- Ticket 3 is open. Neither outcome on #4471 touches a one-day shift, so its five reports wait for
  someone to reproduce the shift across time zones; they are listed in the ticket.
- When a subject-only merge next eats a quiet bug, this page is the precedent for splitting.

